Reason: a destroy rewrites other instances' stale copies of the dead id, and patch 0025 already limits that to
instances flagged as holding id-like numbers, but each flagged instance still had every slot of its variable table
scanned. Enemies, Samus and the effects that remember who made them all hold ids, and their tables are large: on the
real Wii a destroy cost about 0.2 ms (a hit spark's animation end, which only destroys itself, took 0.84 ms a frame
during a beam fight in rm_a5c27). Instance_setSelfVar, the one place an id-like number enters a slot, now also lists
that variable once per instance, and the rewrite looks only at the listed variables, dropping those that no longer
hold an id-like number; the same slots are rewritten as before.
Upstream: Speed only, every platform. Retires this patch when merged.

--- a/src/instance.h
+++ b/src/instance.h
@@ -41,6 +41,8 @@
     bool persistent, solid, active, destroyed, visible, createEventFired, outsideRoom, spatialGridDirty, mouseOver;
     // Set when a self variable is given a number that could be an instance id, so a destroy only scans these instances.
     bool mayHoldIds;
+    // The self variables that were given such a number (varIDs, each once), so a destroy looks only at them.
+    int32_t* idVarKeys;
     // Used to track which alarms are set without looping through the entire alarm array
     uint16_t activeAlarmMask;
     int32_t maskIndex; // collision mask sprite override (-1 = use spriteIndex)
@@ -135,8 +137,11 @@
     RValue independentVal = RValue_makeIndependent(val);
     RValue_free(pointerToSlot);
     *pointerToSlot = independentVal;
-    if (RValue_isIdLike(independentVal))
+    if (RValue_isIdLike(independentVal)) {
         inst->mayHoldIds = true;
+        repeat(arrlen(inst->idVarKeys), i) if (inst->idVarKeys[i] == varID) return;
+        arrput(inst->idVarKeys, varID);
+    }
 }
 
 // Recompute speed/direction from hspeed/vspeed (called when hspeed or vspeed is set)
--- a/src/instance.c
